Q2. Demonstrate ++ and -- operators (both prefix and postfix) for all possible
combinations.
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
 int x = 5, y;
 cout << "Start: x = " << x << endl;
 // Postfix: value is used first, then changed
 y = x++;
 cout << "y = x++ -> y = " << y << ", x = " << x << endl;
 // Prefix: value is changed first, then used
 y = ++x;
 cout << "y = ++x -> y = " << y << ", x = " << x << endl;
 y = x--;
 cout << "y = x-- -> y = " << y << ", x = " << x << endl;
 y = --x;
 cout << "y = --x -> y = " << y << ", x = " << x << endl;
 // Alone on a line, prefix and postfix give the same result
 x++;
 cout << "After x++ : " << x << endl;
 ++x;
 cout << "After ++x : " << x << endl;
 x--;
 cout << "After x-- : " << x << endl;
 --x;
 cout << "After --x : " << x << endl;
 cout << "Name: Prince" << endl;
 return 0;
}
// Name: Prince

Q3. Demonstrate bitwise AND (&), OR (|) on two integers. Discuss their significance in
real life use cases.
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
 int a, b;
 cout << "Enter first number: ";
 cin >> a;
 cout << "Enter second number: ";
 cin >> b;
 // Bitwise operators work on the binary form of numbers.
 // Example: 12 is 1100 and 10 is 1010 in binary.
 cout << "a & b = " << (a & b) << endl;
 cout << "a | b = " << (a | b) << endl;
 cout << "Name: Prince" << endl;
 return 0;
}
// Name: Prince


How it works (12 and 10):
12 = 1100 and 10 = 1010 in binary.
AND (&) gives 1 only where both bits are 1: 1100 & 1010 = 1000 = 8.
OR (|) gives 1 where at least one bit is 1: 1100 | 1010 = 1110 = 14.
Real life uses
AND: checking if a setting is ON (for example, a permission), checking if a number is odd (number & 1).
OR: turning a setting ON, and combining several options into one number (like file permissions).
